.TH CHTTPSVR_ENGINE_STOP 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_engine_stop \- signal the engine to stop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_engine_stop(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Signals chttpserver's own shared reactor (and its companion
idle-timeout sweep thread) to stop, forcing it down even if other
chttpsvr instances in the same process still hold a reference and are
still fully started.  This reactor is independent of chttpclient's own
engine, so calling this function has no effect on any chttpclient
async work (chttpclient_do_async/_streaming,
chttpclient_do_pooled/_streaming) in the same process.  Returns
immediately; use chttpsvr_engine_wait() to block until the stop
completes.

Before the shared reactor is actually torn down, every chttpsvr
instance still registered in the process (regardless of whether it is
still started) is quiesced exactly as chttpsvr_destroy() would quiesce
it on its own: listener closed, in-flight requests drained, idle
connections closed, worker pool shut down, and the instance's own
reference to the engine released.  This makes the documented
signal-handler shutdown pattern below safe even though
chttpsvr_destroy(srv) is called after the engine has already been torn
down: __chttpsvr_destroy is idempotent per server (see its own
documentation), so it only frees the remaining per-server memory at
that point rather than repeating any teardown work.

    static void on_signal(int sig) { (void)sig; chttpsvr_engine_stop(); }
    /* ... */
    chttpsvr_start(srv, &cfg);
    chttpsvr_engine_wait();   /* returns once on_signal fires */
    chttpsvr_destroy(srv);    /* safe: srv was already quiesced above */

Non-blocking and async-signal-safe: it is safe to call this function
directly from a POSIX signal handler (e.g. SIGINT, SIGTERM).

The library does not install any signal handlers.  Applications are
responsible for wiring this function into whatever signal or shutdown
mechanism they use.

Safe to call before the reactor has ever started (no-op).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
